Roast.pet Weird Builder Projects =E2=80=94 5 useful projects

Material delta: the standout is HistOracle-production/blabberer, which
appears to collapse the exact V1 problem we were still planning to
assemble ourselves: portrait =E2=86=92 nine generated viseme sprites =E2=86=
=92
transparent post-processing =E2=86=92 browser lip-sync engine. This is
substantially closer to Roast.pet than generic LivePortrait or Rhubarb
wrappers.

1) Blabberer =E2=80=94 REUSE NOW / FORK

URL: https://github.com/HistOracle-production/blabberer

Creator: HistOracle / Editlingo Solutions LLC

Activity: public 2026 project; indexed in the latest scan

License: MIT

Solves: automatically creates 9 Preston-Blair mouth shapes from a
single portrait, detects the face with MediaPipe, generates mouth
variants through Gemini/OpenRouter, outputs transparent feathered WebP
sprites, and includes a zero-framework TypeScript real-time lip-sync
engine.

Route: Python sprite-generation pipeline + demo/npm install && npm run
dev; standalone TS engine can be copied directly.

Dependencies/hardware: MediaPipe, image-generation API for sprite
creation; browser Web Audio for playback. No dedicated GPU required
for the lip-sync runtime.

Evidence: repository documents the production pipeline extracted from
HistOracle, an interactive demo, nine-shape output, face
auto-detection, dual-layer crossfade renderer, and 60fps
audio/text-informed mouth-state engine.

Work replaced: face crop detection + mouth asset generation +
postprocessing + mouth-state engine + crossfade renderer =E2=80=94 potentia=
lly
most of Roast.pet V1 facial animation.

Difficulty: LOW-MEDIUM. Maintenance risk: LOW-MEDIUM.

Experiment: run one canonical dog podium image and one cat through the
pipeline. The human MediaPipe crop may fail on pets; if so, bypass
detection with our existing pet crop and test only the nine-sprite
generator/postprocessor/runtime. Compare against meme-lipsync and
Catsync on identity retention and comedy.

2) ScratchLipSyncTool =E2=80=94 COPY MECHANISM

URL: https://github.com/griffpatch/ScratchLipSyncTool

Creator: griffpatch

Activity: indexed within the last month

License: MIT

Solves: browser speech audio =E2=86=92 phonemes =E2=86=92 visemes =E2=86=92=
 talking sprite,
using PocketSphinx.js.

Route: runnable GitHub Pages browser app; JS source is small enough to
inspect directly.

Dependencies/hardware: browser only; PocketSphinx.js; no GPU.

Evidence: live browser tool and Scratch-ready talking-sprite export.

Work replaced: possible server-side Rhubarb dependency for simple
English roast speech.

Difficulty: LOW. Maintenance risk: MEDIUM because PocketSphinx is older.

Experiment: feed the same 10 Kokoro roast lines to ScratchLipSyncTool
and Rhubarb. Compare viseme timing. If acceptable, this gives a fully
browser-side phoneme path.

3) BorderCut =E2=80=94 REUSE NOW for easy photos

URL: https://github.com/kong75/bordercut

Creator: kong75

Activity: indexed last week

License: inspect package/repo license before production integration

Solves: background removal for separable backgrounds in ~8 kB gzipped
with zero model/runtime dependencies.

Route: @bordercut/core in browser/Worker/Node; also Dart/Flutter.

Dependencies/hardware: none; offline; no GPU/model download.

Evidence: 7.9 kB gzipped TypeScript core, 8.7 kB browser adapter,
explicit browser/Node support and Remove/Keep guidance for difficult
images.

Work replaced: running a 50=E2=80=93500 MB matting model on the large fract=
ion
of customer photos with clean backgrounds.

Difficulty: LOW. Maintenance risk: LOW.

Experiment: route our 30-photo customer torture set through BorderCut
first, then fall back to Alphaveil only on low-confidence/visually bad
results. Measure what percentage avoids model loading entirely.

4) PICORN Background Remover =E2=80=94 WATCH / BENCHMARK

URL: https://github.com/cueqzapper/PICORN-Background-Remover

Creator: cueqzapper

Activity: indexed within the last month

License: browser code MIT; model weights have separate terms and
require commercial licensing for commercial/hosted use.

Solves: tiny browser neural matte + deterministic edge refinement +
manual restore/remove brush.

Route: Vite app / ONNX Runtime Web; WebGPU with WASM fallback.

Dependencies/hardware: 1.35 MB ONNX model, browser only.

Evidence: model has ~313k parameters; soft alpha output; guided edge
refinement; manual trimap refinement; tests and live demo.

Work replaced: heavyweight cutout model plus much of the correction UI.

Difficulty: LOW technically. Maintenance/licensing risk: MEDIUM-HIGH
because commercial model rights are separate.

Experiment: benchmark fur edges versus Alphaveil/Cutout Studio on 10
hard pets. Do not integrate until model commercial terms are
acceptable.

5) Light Converter =E2=80=94 COPY MECHANISM

URL: https://github.com/lightScout/light-converter

Creator: lightScout

Activity: indexed 2026-09-15

License: MIT

Solves: batch browser cutout with a particularly useful automatic
second pass for uncertain masks.

Route: Astro/Svelte/onnxruntime-web; WebGPU BiRefNet and WASM IS-Net fallba=
ck.

Dependencies/hardware: browser WebGPU preferred; WASM fallback.

Evidence: automatically triggers a CLAHE contrast-boosted second pass
when the first mask is uncertain; guided-filter refinement, hole/speck
cleanup, restore/erase brushes, IndexedDB batch persistence.

Work replaced: quality-gating/retry logic around customer cutouts, not
just the segmentation model itself.

Difficulty: LOW-MEDIUM. Maintenance risk: LOW-MEDIUM.

Experiment: copy only the uncertainty =E2=86=92 contrast-enhanced retry
strategy into the preprocessing benchmark. Specifically test
white/light pets against white walls and black pets in dark interiors.

TOP 5 THINGS WE SHOULD NOT BUILD OURSELVES

- Nine-mouth sprite generation: test Blabberer first.

- Lip-sync state engine/crossfade renderer: Blabberer=E2=80=99s standalone
TypeScript engine.

- Phoneme recognition: Rhubarb already works; ScratchLipSyncTool is a
browser-only fallback worth benchmarking.

- Easy-photo background removal: BorderCut before loading ML.

- Hard-photo retry/refinement logic: copy Light Converter=E2=80=99s
uncertainty-triggered second pass rather than inventing our own.

Most surprising hobby project

Blabberer. It is almost exactly the missing bridge between =E2=80=9Ccanonic=
al
pet image=E2=80=9D and =E2=80=9Cnine reusable mouth states.=E2=80=9D The ke=
y experiment is
whether its image-generation prompts/postprocessing generalize to pets
when we supply the crop ourselves.

Fastest integration win

Blabberer=E2=80=99s TypeScript LipSyncEngine. It is standalone,
framework-free, uses audio energy plus text-informed mouth
preferences, hysteresis, minimum hold times and crossfades. We can
test it against our existing pet sprite assets without adopting the
rest of the project.

Repo/author worth following

HistOracle-production / Editlingo. Their production need=E2=80=94turn stati=
c
historical portraits into cheap interactive talking characters=E2=80=94is
structurally very close to Roast.pet=E2=80=99s need, so adjacent work is
unusually likely to transfer.
